course: biology
lesson: 213
title: Ducks, geese and farm myths
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A duck can paddle about all day in a winter pond and come out with dry feathers and warm insides, and that is two clever tricks at once.
explain: First, it rubs oil from a gland near its tail all over its feathers with its beak, so water runs off like rain off a raincoat, which is where «like water off a duck's back» comes from, a saying for something, such as a telling-off, that has no effect on someone.
explain: Second, the warm blood going down its legs passes right next to the cold blood coming back up, and hands over its heat, so the feet stay cold and lose little heat while the body stays warm.
explain: Ducks and geese, like many farm animals, are also wrapped in myths, from quacks that never echo to ostriches hiding their heads, and a bit of science puts them right.
fact: Ducks can swim in icy water because warm and cold blood vessels in their legs swap heat, so their feet stay cold and their body stays warm, and their feet do not stick to ice.
fact: A duck spreads oil from a gland near its tail over its feathers with its beak, which keeps them waterproof.
fact: White bread is bad for ducks: it fills them up without the goodness they need, and soggy leftover bread pollutes ponds; peas, sweetcorn, oats and duck food are better.
fact: Ducklings follow the first moving thing they see after hatching, usually their mother; this is called imprinting, and ducklings have imprinted on people, dogs and even boots.
fact: A duck with its head under and tail up is dabbling, feeding on plants just under the surface, while diving ducks go completely underwater.
fact: The saying that a duck's quack does not echo is a myth: scientists tested it in a special sound room in 2003 and found that it does echo, though the echo is quiet and hard to notice.
fact: Geese defend their nests fiercely and hiss at people; they are big and brave and have been kept as guards, and an old Roman story tells of geese warning of a night attack.
fact: Geese fly in a V because each bird catches rising air from the wingtip of the one in front, which saves energy, and they take turns at the tiring front place.
fact: Many cow breeds are brown, red or black; only some dairy breeds such as the Holstein are black and white, and each cow's pattern is unique like a fingerprint.
fact: Ostriches do not bury their heads in the sand: they lie flat to hide and turn their eggs in a nest dug in the ground, which from far away can look like heads in the sand.
fact: An ostrich in danger runs at up to about seventy kilometres an hour or kicks hard.
fact: Only male turkeys gobble, to attract females and warn rivals; wild turkeys can fly and run fast, but farm turkeys are bred so heavy that they can hardly fly.
exercise: Why don't a duck's feet freeze in icy water? | Blood vessels in its legs swap heat, keeping the body warm | Ducks have thick warm fur on their feet, like a cat | Ducks' feet are made of plastic-like skin with no blood at all inside | Ducks only swim for one minute at a time
exercise: How does a duck keep its feathers waterproof? | It spreads oil from a gland near its tail | It dries them in the sun every hour of the day | Its feathers are made of wax | It shakes off every single drop of water as soon as it gets out
exercise: What does «like water off a duck's back» mean? | Something has no effect on someone | Someone is very wet | Someone is a good swimmer | Something is very cold
exercise: Why is white bread bad for ducks? | It fills them up without the goodness they need | It makes their feathers fall out in clumps every spring | It turns their beaks white | It is too heavy for them to swim
exercise: Which food is better to give ducks? | Peas, sweetcorn or oats | Crisps and biscuits | Chocolate cake | Mouldy white bread
exercise: Why do ducklings follow their mother in a line? | They imprint on the first moving thing they see | The mother ties them together with a long piece of string | They are following her smell only | They are scared of the water
exercise: What is imprinting? | A newly hatched bird learning to follow the first moving thing it sees | A duck printing its footprints in soft mud so other ducks can follow it | A mother duck marking her eggs | A duckling learning to swim
exercise: What is a duck doing with its head under and its tail up? | Dabbling for plants just under the surface | Trying to drown | Hiding from a hawk that is flying over the pond | Sleeping upside down
exercise: How are diving ducks different from dabbling ducks? | Diving ducks go completely underwater | Diving ducks never get wet, because of their oil | Diving ducks only eat bread thrown from the bank | Diving ducks cannot swim at all, only walk on land
exercise: Does a duck's quack echo? | Yes, but the echo is quiet and hard to notice | No, it is the only sound in the world with no echo at all | Only on Tuesdays | Only underwater
exercise: Why does a goose hiss at people? | It is defending its nest | It is hungry and wants your bread | It is singing a song to you | It has a cold and a sore throat
exercise: Why have geese been used as guards? | They are big, brave and loud when strangers come | They can bite through metal and wood | They can call the police | They can see through walls and doors to spot burglars hiding
exercise: Why do geese fly in a V shape? | Each catches rising air from the wingtip in front, saving energy | So they can talk to each other and plan where to land for the night | To spell a word in the sky | So the leader can see them all
exercise: Why do geese take turns at the front of the V? | The front place is the most tiring | The front goose gets the most food | The front goose is always the oldest | They want to see where they are going first
exercise: Are all farm cows black and white? | No, many breeds are brown, red or black | Yes, every cow on every farm | Only in cartoons are cows brown | Only bulls are black and white
exercise: Why can it look as if ostriches bury their heads in the sand? | They lie flat to hide and turn eggs in a nest in the ground | They really do bury their heads | They eat sand all day | They sleep with their heads underground so that lions cannot see them at night
exercise: What does a frightened ostrich really do? | Runs very fast or kicks hard | Buries its head deep in the sand | Climbs the nearest tree to hide | Flies away over the bushes to safety
exercise: Why can't farm turkeys fly well? | They are bred so heavy | They have no wings at all | They forget how to fly as chicks | Farmers glue their wings
test: «my teacher said a duck's quack doesn't echo, mad innit» What is right? | It is a myth; tests showed the quack does echo, just quietly | Yes, it is the only sound in the world that cannot echo, scientists say | Yes, because ducks quack underwater | No, a quack echoes only in caves
test: «mum duck and 8 ducklings walking in a line, how many legs» | 18 | 16 | 36 | 9
test: «2 geese, 3 ducks and a turkey on the farm. count the wings, no trunks obviously» | 12 | 10 | 6 | 24
test: «chuck them the old bread, ducks love it» What is better? | Peas, sweetcorn or oats, because bread fills them up with little goodness | Bread is the best food for ducks | Crisps are better than bread | Ducks should never be fed anything at all, not even peas, or they forget how to find food
test: In a cartoon an ostrich panics and sticks its head in the sand. What does a real ostrich do in danger? | Runs at up to seventy kilometres an hour or kicks | Buries its head deep in the sand until all the danger has passed by | Lies on its back and plays dead forever | Climbs into its nest and hides its head
test: «every cow in my picture book is black and white with a pink nose» What is right? | Many cow breeds are brown, red or black | All cows in the world are black and white | Black and white cows are only in cartoons | Brown cows are just dirty black and white ones
test: «get away from that goose, it's hissing at ya!» Why is it hissing? | It is defending its nest | It wants to be stroked on the neck | It is laughing at the way you walk | It is cold and wants to get warm
test: What would happen if a duck's oil gland stopped working? | Its feathers would soak up water and it could get cold and heavy | It would swim faster | It would turn into a goose | Nothing at all would happen, because ducks do not need oil to stay dry and warm
test: A gosling, a baby goose, hatches in a farmer's kitchen and the first thing it sees is the farmer's boots. What may happen? | It may follow the boots around as if they were its mother | It will run away from the boots | It will learn to talk | Nothing, it ignores everything that moves and waits for a duck or goose to come
test: «why's that turkey going gobble gobble, is it a boy or a girl» What is right? | Only males gobble, so it is a male | Only females gobble | Both gobble the same | Turkeys never gobble
test: «why's the front goose always changing» What is right? | They take turns because the front is most tiring | The front goose keeps getting lost, so another one takes over the map | They are racing to be first | The front goose falls asleep
test: Donald Duck in cartoons walks about on the ice all winter in a sailor's top. What keeps a real duck warm on ice? | Heat swapped between blood vessels in its legs and its waterproof feathers | A jacket of thick fur | Ducks feel no cold at all | Ducks keep warm by quacking loudly and flapping their wings all night on the ice
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the means whereby ducks maintain bodily warmth in icy water? | Heat is exchanged between warm and cold blood vessels in the legs, the body remaining warm | Ducks possess fur upon their feet | Ducks are insensible to cold by reason of their quacking, which warms the blood throughout the body | The feet of ducks contain no blood whatsoever
exam: Identify the principal reason for which geese fly in a V formation. | Each bird derives lift from the air rising off the wingtip of the bird preceding it, thereby conserving energy | The formation permits communication by gesture | The formation spells a letter for observers below | The leading bird alone knows the destination, the others following it closely so as not to lose the way
exam: It is given that a newly hatched duckling follows a person in preference to its mother. Ascertain the most probable explanation. | The duckling imprinted upon the first moving object it perceived | The person resembles a duck in plumage | Ducklings follow whoever carries bread | The mother has instructed the duckling to do so
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the ostrich and the concealment of its head. | Ostriches do not bury their heads, but lie flat to hide and attend to eggs in a ground nest | Ostriches bury their heads whenever danger approaches, remaining so until the danger has departed | Ostriches sleep with their heads beneath the sand | Ostriches consume sand as their principal food
